WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.301 --> 00:00:02.368
-Hi, everybody.
Welcome to another edition

00:00:02.368 --> 00:00:04.370
of "The Tonight Show"
At-Home Edition.

00:00:04.370 --> 00:00:06.605
Today is Tuesday.

00:00:06.605 --> 00:00:10.243
And, um, we're just
kind of in this now.

00:00:10.243 --> 00:00:14.113
So I hope everyone
is getting in a routine

00:00:14.113 --> 00:00:17.150
and kind of getting their life

00:00:17.150 --> 00:00:20.586
kind of to a schedule, a rhythm.

00:00:20.586 --> 00:00:22.188
Yesterday
it was just rainy here,

00:00:22.188 --> 00:00:25.091
so any little ray of sun,
I'm going to take it

00:00:25.091 --> 00:00:26.992
and just sit in the backyard

00:00:26.992 --> 00:00:30.530
and do a television show,
just like everyone should do.

00:00:30.530 --> 00:00:32.432
I just want to be here
for you guys,

00:00:32.432 --> 00:00:34.267
'cause you've always
been there for me.

00:00:34.267 --> 00:00:37.203
So I appreciate it, guys.
We have a fun show tonight.

00:00:37.203 --> 00:00:38.838
He has a movie
called "The Quarry."

00:00:38.838 --> 00:00:41.674
It's out on demand on Friday.
Michael Shannon is here.

00:00:41.674 --> 00:00:43.977
The very fun, the very odd
Michael Shannon.

00:00:43.977 --> 00:00:46.479
He's great tonight.
Oh, my gosh.

00:00:46.479 --> 00:00:50.650
Also, my friend J.J. Watt
is on the show, as well,

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:51.951
and I'm going to play
"Know Your Bro"

00:00:51.951 --> 00:00:54.386
with his brothers --
some family trivia

00:00:54.386 --> 00:00:57.123
about his brothers,
who are all playing in the NFL.

00:00:57.123 --> 00:00:59.692
Pretty good family. Huh?
That's pretty awesome.

00:00:59.692 --> 00:01:02.762
And then to close it out,
one of my super favorites.

00:01:02.762 --> 00:01:04.764
Remember she almost
hopped our fence once?

00:01:04.764 --> 00:01:06.199
Rita Ora is on the show tonight.

00:01:06.199 --> 00:01:08.334
-[ Laughs ] So cool.
-Yeah.

00:01:08.334 --> 00:01:10.770
So I'll be talking -- Well,
I won't be talking to Rita.

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:14.040
She'll be performing a new song
off her upcoming album.

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:17.811
I should say Michael Shannon's
charity is earthday.org.

00:01:22.582 --> 00:01:26.553
This says --
Feeding America is J.J. Watt.

00:01:29.555 --> 00:01:35.661
And covid19responsefund.org
is Rita Ora.

00:01:35.661 --> 00:01:39.899
Uh, let's try to make you laugh,
and let's have a good show.

00:01:39.899 --> 00:01:41.834
It's time for the monologue!

00:01:41.834 --> 00:01:44.036
♪♪

00:01:44.036 --> 00:01:45.304
All right.
Welcome to "The Tonight Show."

00:01:45.304 --> 00:01:46.572
I'm Jimmy Fallon.

00:01:46.572 --> 00:01:49.308
Well, guys, earlier today,
former President Obama

00:01:49.308 --> 00:01:51.177
endorsed Joe Biden
for president.

00:01:51.177 --> 00:01:53.680
Obama said he knew Biden
was the right candidate

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:56.716
once he was absolutely sure
Michelle wasn't running.

00:01:58.751 --> 00:02:00.620
You can tell the endorsement
meant a lot to Biden.

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:03.522
Right after, he was like,
"I know you guys really love me

00:02:03.522 --> 00:02:09.829
because I only had to ask
every day for two years."

00:02:09.829 --> 00:02:11.564
I like how Obama
endorsed Biden now.

00:02:11.564 --> 00:02:13.800
That's like waiting
until there's one second left

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:15.434
in the Super Bowl
and then saying,

00:02:15.434 --> 00:02:17.503
"Okay, I'm rooting
for the Chiefs.

00:02:17.503 --> 00:02:20.673
Okay? Just for the record."

00:02:20.673 --> 00:02:22.875
This isn't good --
Healthy Choice recalled

00:02:22.875 --> 00:02:26.512
over 130,000 pounds
of frozen chicken meals

00:02:26.512 --> 00:02:30.382
after customers
found small rocks mixed in.

00:02:30.382 --> 00:02:31.618
Americans were like,

00:02:31.618 --> 00:02:33.418
"We make one healthy choice
in five weeks,

00:02:33.418 --> 00:02:36.288
and this is what happens?

00:02:36.288 --> 00:02:39.725
We're eating rocks?"

00:02:39.725 --> 00:02:42.128
Let me tell you guys something.
With this toilet-paper shortage,

00:02:42.128 --> 00:02:46.366
this is not a good time
to have rocks in your system.

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:50.469
People who ate Healthy Choice
meals filled with rocks

00:02:50.469 --> 00:02:53.940
knew something was up when their
toilet looked like a fish tank.

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:56.776
[ Chuckles ]

00:02:56.776 --> 00:02:59.511
Burger King just introduced
a new sandwich

00:02:59.511 --> 00:03:01.714
called the Roasted Garlic King,

00:03:01.714 --> 00:03:04.616
which is a burger
with a thick garlic sauce.

00:03:04.616 --> 00:03:06.819
Yeah. Burger King said,
"If this doesn't keep people

00:03:06.819 --> 00:03:10.489
six feet away, nothing will."

00:03:10.489 --> 00:03:12.524
During quarantine,
many Americans are watching

00:03:12.524 --> 00:03:15.828
old hit TV series like
"The Wire" and "Breaking Bad."

00:03:15.828 --> 00:03:17.964
Mostly to finally shut up
that annoying friend

00:03:17.964 --> 00:03:21.301
who's always like,
"You've never seen 'The Wire'?

00:03:23.769 --> 00:03:28.241
Aw, bro.
You never seen 'The Wire'?

00:03:28.241 --> 00:03:31.144
Aww. Bro.

00:03:31.144 --> 00:03:33.513
'The Wire'?!

00:03:33.513 --> 00:03:35.948
You never seen it?

00:03:35.948 --> 00:03:38.784
Not the third episode?

00:03:38.784 --> 00:03:41.154
Or the second one?
Or the first one?

00:03:41.154 --> 00:03:44.090
'The wire'?"

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:45.925
I read that many brides-to-be

00:03:45.925 --> 00:03:48.294
are now having
virtual bachelorette parties.

00:03:48.294 --> 00:03:50.329
It starts with
some lemon-drop shots

00:03:50.329 --> 00:03:53.999
and ends with virtually
everyone crying in the bathroom.

00:03:53.999 --> 00:03:55.735
That's right --
brides-to-be are having

00:03:55.735 --> 00:03:57.603
virtual bachelorette parties.

00:03:57.603 --> 00:03:59.706
It's a lot of fun right up
until your friend Debbie

00:03:59.706 --> 00:04:04.176
says something vaguely racist
to the virtual limo driver.

00:04:04.176 --> 00:04:05.912
And finally,
here's an update on a story

00:04:05.912 --> 00:04:07.480
we've been following
since it broke.

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:10.049
A 93-year-old Pennsylvania woman
who went viral

00:04:10.049 --> 00:04:12.485
with her sign that said
"I need more beer"

00:04:12.485 --> 00:04:15.321
received 150 beers
from Coors Light.

00:04:15.321 --> 00:04:16.956
After getting the Coors Light,

00:04:16.956 --> 00:04:18.825
the woman held up
a new sign that said,

00:04:18.825 --> 00:04:20.826
"I said I need beer, not water."

00:04:20.826 --> 00:04:24.229
[ Laughs ]

00:04:24.229 --> 00:04:26.332
It was a nice moment
until an hour later

00:04:26.332 --> 00:04:28.968
when she held up a sign
that said "out of beer again."

00:04:28.968 --> 00:04:30.803
Hey, you got
a problem there, pal.

00:04:30.803 --> 00:04:32.605
That's our monologue
right there.

00:04:32.605 --> 00:04:34.841
Guys, we had a lot of fun with
#AskTheFallons

00:04:34.841 --> 00:04:36.676
where you guys
are sending questions

00:04:36.676 --> 00:04:39.511
that you'd like me to ask
my wife or my kids.

00:04:39.511 --> 00:04:43.348
And so tonight I'm going to ask
a question from you guys

00:04:43.348 --> 00:04:47.086
to my wife, who's very --
who's also operating my camera

00:04:47.086 --> 00:04:49.555
and, really, producing
this -- my whole show.

00:04:49.555 --> 00:04:51.156
I love you.

00:04:51.156 --> 00:04:54.560
And she also turns the tables
and asks me a question

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:57.196
in this part of
"Ask the Fallons."

00:04:57.196 --> 00:04:58.731
Enjoy.

00:04:58.731 --> 00:05:01.133
♪♪

00:05:01.133 --> 00:05:04.170
Here we go.
This is "Ask the Fallons."

00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:06.605
#AskTheFallons.
I said it like I'm angry.

00:05:06.605 --> 00:05:08.307
-[ Laughs ]
I think you might be.

00:05:08.307 --> 00:05:09.642
-I'm not angry.

00:05:09.642 --> 00:05:11.577
-Guess whose turn it is
to be interviewed.

00:05:11.577 --> 00:05:18.284
-No. All right.
This one is from @jusouzdiaz.

00:05:18.284 --> 00:05:21.988
"Jimmy, how did you
propose to Nancy?"

00:05:21.988 --> 00:05:24.122
Well, we told this story,
I believe.

00:05:24.122 --> 00:05:25.924
Well, we definitely have.

00:05:25.924 --> 00:05:29.061
But I had the ring ready to go.

00:05:29.061 --> 00:05:32.264
Weirdly, I had the ring
from Neil Lane.

00:05:32.264 --> 00:05:33.766
Oh, sorry.
You're not even on the camera.

00:05:33.766 --> 00:05:35.634
-[ Laughs ]

00:05:35.634 --> 00:05:38.470
-Neil Lane,
who's now "Bachelor" famous.

00:05:38.470 --> 00:05:41.073
I got the ring.

00:05:41.073 --> 00:05:44.644
I remember I called your dad
for permission.

00:05:44.644 --> 00:05:47.146
I asked him. He said, "Yes."

00:05:47.146 --> 00:05:48.981
-Sweet.

00:05:48.981 --> 00:05:52.485
-And I asked your brother,
as well.

00:05:52.485 --> 00:05:54.754
-Aww! Jim.
-Yeah.

00:05:54.754 --> 00:05:56.322
Or told him maybe.

00:05:56.322 --> 00:05:57.790
Informed him
that I talked to your dad.

00:05:57.790 --> 00:06:00.025
Why would I ask your brother?
-I don't know. Sweet, though.

00:06:00.025 --> 00:06:01.226
-Yeah, I might have.

00:06:01.226 --> 00:06:03.096
But your brother
was cool with it, too.

00:06:03.096 --> 00:06:06.932
And then what happened was you
messed everything up for me.

00:06:06.932 --> 00:06:08.200
-Yes.
-You get to the door,

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:09.935
and you go,
"I have a surprise for you."

00:06:09.935 --> 00:06:11.837
And I go, "I hope it doesn't
mess up my surprise,"

00:06:11.837 --> 00:06:13.338
which was the big thing.

00:06:13.338 --> 00:06:15.341
I got the ring, I got the --
So, you go,

00:06:15.341 --> 00:06:18.845
"I got us reservations
at Per Se,"

00:06:18.845 --> 00:06:20.880
this amazing restaurant
in New York.

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:23.115
And I go, "Oh, my gosh."
I was dying to go to Per Se.

00:06:23.115 --> 00:06:25.517
And I go, "Oh, my gosh.
This is a big deal.

00:06:25.517 --> 00:06:27.753
I really do
have to go to Per Se."

00:06:27.753 --> 00:06:31.323
But I didn't want to get engaged
at the restaurant.

00:06:31.323 --> 00:06:33.358
I never wanted --
I was going to get engaged in,

00:06:33.358 --> 00:06:36.662
like, Gramercy Park or something
that will just always be around,

00:06:36.662 --> 00:06:37.863
something classic.

00:06:37.863 --> 00:06:39.398
I don't want
to go to a restaurant

00:06:39.398 --> 00:06:41.501
'cause what if in 30 years
the restaurant changes hands?

00:06:41.501 --> 00:06:42.901
Now it's a laser-tag place.

00:06:42.901 --> 00:06:44.870
Now it's like, "Kids,
this is where your mom and I

00:06:44.870 --> 00:06:46.806
got engaged,
at the Laser Tag House,"

00:06:46.806 --> 00:06:48.841
if they do have laser tag
in the future.

00:06:48.841 --> 00:06:51.577
Anyway, so, I go,
"I'll do it after dinner."

00:06:51.577 --> 00:06:53.679
I didn't mention it to you.
So, we went to dinner.

00:06:53.679 --> 00:06:56.215
It was an amazing dinner. Right?
-Yeah.

00:06:56.215 --> 00:06:57.950
-Cut to --
-It was a long, long dinner.

00:06:57.950 --> 00:07:00.352
-Cut to -- I'm not kidding --
14 courses later.

00:07:00.352 --> 00:07:01.820
-It was wild.

00:07:01.820 --> 00:07:04.656
-And then we got home,
and your brother texted

00:07:04.656 --> 00:07:05.891
or called and said, like,

00:07:05.891 --> 00:07:07.359
"Did you do it, man?
What's up?"

00:07:07.359 --> 00:07:10.329
I go, "No, man, our dinner was
like four hours or something."

00:07:10.329 --> 00:07:12.097
He goes, "You got to do it.

00:07:12.097 --> 00:07:14.834
Just go to Molly's or something.
And do it."

00:07:14.834 --> 00:07:17.069
-God. Yes. Which --
We love Molly's.

00:07:17.069 --> 00:07:19.204
-Yeah. And so I was like,
"Oh, all right.

00:07:19.204 --> 00:07:21.073
Maybe I'll go to Molly's."

00:07:21.073 --> 00:07:22.541
Hold on. I was in the bathroom.

00:07:22.541 --> 00:07:25.678
I go, "Honey, you want to go out
and have one more drink?"

00:07:25.678 --> 00:07:29.782
And you go, "Are you -- Really?
are you serious? I will."

00:07:29.782 --> 00:07:31.517
You're like,
"I'm ready to, like, watch TV

00:07:31.517 --> 00:07:33.318
and, like, go to bed."
-Yeah.

00:07:33.318 --> 00:07:36.088
-So, that's what we did.

00:07:36.088 --> 00:07:38.257
And then it wasn't for, like --

00:07:38.257 --> 00:07:42.828
It was probably three months
later, six months later?

00:07:42.828 --> 00:07:44.764
-It was I think longer

00:07:44.764 --> 00:07:48.701
than your parents
and my parents wanted.

00:07:48.701 --> 00:07:51.471
But, of course, yeah,
I wasn't thinking it was --

00:07:51.471 --> 00:07:53.772
I don't know.
-It was probably -- Yeah.

00:07:53.772 --> 00:07:55.675
It was probably --
Yeah, no, you didn't know.

00:07:55.675 --> 00:07:57.476
-I just knew we were
going to be together.

00:07:57.476 --> 00:07:59.779
It wasn't like we got to
get married or not get married.

00:07:59.779 --> 00:08:01.480
-Anyways, like, I want
to say four months later

00:08:01.480 --> 00:08:03.315
I was just trying to find
the right place to do it.

00:08:03.315 --> 00:08:05.251
I know, I know, I know.
But I'm very, like, particular.

00:08:05.251 --> 00:08:06.719
I got to make sure
it's the right spot.

00:08:06.719 --> 00:08:08.854
-This is going to feed
into my question.

00:08:08.854 --> 00:08:10.689
-Ooh.
-[ Laughs ]

00:08:10.689 --> 00:08:11.924
-So, we get to --

00:08:11.924 --> 00:08:14.994
Your family has a summer home
in New Hampshire.

00:08:14.994 --> 00:08:16.695
In Wolfeboro, New Hampshire,
which we love.

00:08:16.695 --> 00:08:18.330
Lake Winnipesaukee.

00:08:18.330 --> 00:08:20.933
And I go to the dock with you.

00:08:20.933 --> 00:08:22.501
Sunset.
-Mm-hmm.

00:08:22.501 --> 00:08:24.369
-I go, "Honey, do you want
to have a cocktail

00:08:24.369 --> 00:08:26.305
and maybe sunset?"
So, we get out.

00:08:26.305 --> 00:08:28.374
I played Harry Nelson.
-Mm-hmm.

00:08:28.374 --> 00:08:31.309
-And I said, okay, in my head,

00:08:31.309 --> 00:08:35.081
"Don't cry. Let her cry first
if someone will cry."

00:08:35.081 --> 00:08:36.682
-[ Laughs ]

00:08:36.682 --> 00:08:40.119
-Because -- Well, because
I hyped it up in my head.

00:08:40.119 --> 00:08:43.188
I get very emotional. I'm Irish.
Very emotional person.

00:08:43.188 --> 00:08:45.123
-I love it.
-So, as soon as I even

00:08:45.123 --> 00:08:47.526
remotely pulled the ring
out of my pocket...

00:08:47.526 --> 00:08:49.495
[ Laughs ]
...I started crying.

00:08:49.495 --> 00:08:51.463
-Yes.
-And I get down on one knee.

00:08:51.463 --> 00:08:55.367
And I'm, like, shaking.
I'm like, "Wah, wah, wah."

00:08:55.367 --> 00:08:56.569
My voice is, like, quivering.

00:08:56.569 --> 00:08:58.203
Looks like
I'm having a heart attack.

00:08:58.203 --> 00:08:59.905
And Nancy's like,
"Are you okay, dude?

00:08:59.905 --> 00:09:01.640
Are you going to be all right?"

00:09:01.640 --> 00:09:04.610
And then I just said,
"Hey, will you marry me?"

00:09:04.610 --> 00:09:06.979
And you said, "Yes."

00:09:06.979 --> 00:09:09.081
-And then the whole family
started cheering

00:09:09.081 --> 00:09:11.684
from inside the house.
-Oh, they were all watching us.

00:09:11.684 --> 00:09:14.052
-Yeah, and somewhere
my brother has this on tape.

00:09:14.052 --> 00:09:16.689
But he said he wouldn't give it
to us for like 20 years.

00:09:16.689 --> 00:09:18.957
-Oh, for real?
-So we're 15 into that.

00:09:18.957 --> 00:09:21.627
We're going to get it
in, like, a few --

00:09:21.627 --> 00:09:23.929
Yeah, like,
five or seven years now.

00:09:23.929 --> 00:09:26.365
-Wow. I didn't know he taped it.
-Yeah.

00:09:26.365 --> 00:09:28.166
-All right.
Let's switch positions.

00:09:28.166 --> 00:09:30.235
And then you ask a question.
-Okay.

00:09:30.235 --> 00:09:32.637
Here we go.
-Okay.

00:09:32.637 --> 00:09:34.173
All right. Here we go.

00:09:34.173 --> 00:09:36.208
Now, this is -- You're
going to ask me a question?

00:09:36.208 --> 00:09:38.077
-Well, I was going to do
interview questions.

00:09:38.077 --> 00:09:40.645
So, I just came up with a few.

00:09:40.645 --> 00:09:43.181
But my first one was
going to be this easy one.

00:09:43.181 --> 00:09:44.650
-Mm-hmm.

00:09:44.650 --> 00:09:47.586
-And it's easy for a Virgo,
especially.

00:09:47.586 --> 00:09:49.388
I was going to ask you

00:09:49.388 --> 00:09:53.058
what only three of your
greatest pet peeves are.

00:09:53.058 --> 00:09:54.893
Only three.
-[ Laughs ]

00:09:54.893 --> 00:09:57.229
-That's more the challenge
than answering this question.

00:09:57.229 --> 00:09:58.998
-I don't even know
if I have a pet peeve.

00:09:58.998 --> 00:10:02.034
-Oh, really?
[ Both laugh ]

00:10:02.034 --> 00:10:04.169
-What?
-Oh, my gosh.

00:10:04.169 --> 00:10:06.806
-Well, I don't like soft cheese.
-Nope. Mnh-mnh.

00:10:06.806 --> 00:10:08.440
-So, if someone --
-And almost anyone

00:10:08.440 --> 00:10:09.909
even eating soft cheese

00:10:09.909 --> 00:10:11.977
is sort of repellent
to you slightly.

00:10:11.977 --> 00:10:13.346
-No, no, no.
I'm totally fine

00:10:13.346 --> 00:10:14.914
with everyone doing
what they want,

00:10:14.914 --> 00:10:17.149
glopping the stuff on the thing
and glopping it in their mouth.

00:10:17.149 --> 00:10:18.884
-"Glopping and glopping."
Yeah.

00:10:18.884 --> 00:10:20.752
That's just no judgment,
though, at all.

00:10:20.752 --> 00:10:22.621
[ Laughs ] Okay. Next.

00:10:22.621 --> 00:10:24.556
-All right. That's one.
That's not bad.

00:10:24.556 --> 00:10:26.458
That's kind of understandable.
-Uh-huh.

00:10:26.458 --> 00:10:28.060
-I think.
-All right.

00:10:28.060 --> 00:10:29.728
-I can't think of any other
pet peeves right now.

00:10:29.728 --> 00:10:32.431
-You have so many, but
I will just let that one ride.

00:10:32.431 --> 00:10:36.135
So, who has been someone
incredibly influential

00:10:36.135 --> 00:10:38.103
in your life?

00:10:38.103 --> 00:10:40.739
-So many people. I don't know.

00:10:40.739 --> 00:10:43.608
Lee. Lorne.
-Mm-hmm.

00:10:43.608 --> 00:10:45.544
-I like those guys.
I don't know. I call them.

00:10:45.544 --> 00:10:48.513
I mean, Lorne Michaels has kind
of seen -- He's seen it all.

00:10:48.513 --> 00:10:50.515
But he's seen me
through the beginning

00:10:50.515 --> 00:10:52.017
of my career through now.

00:10:52.017 --> 00:10:54.520
-You've worked with him
since you were --

00:10:54.520 --> 00:10:56.622
-23 is when I got
"Saturday Night Live."

00:10:56.622 --> 00:11:01.360
I'm 45 now. So it's 22 years
of working together.

00:11:01.360 --> 00:11:03.029
-Lorne Michaels.

00:11:03.029 --> 00:11:05.464
-He's got great advice
about everything.

00:11:05.464 --> 00:11:07.733
-He has great
anecdotal advice, too.

00:11:07.733 --> 00:11:09.501
-Yeah. And he's --
But even, like, anything.

00:11:09.501 --> 00:11:11.203
If I'm renting a car,
I would call Lorne

00:11:11.203 --> 00:11:14.473
and say, "Hey, I don't know
what to do. I'm renting a car."

00:11:14.473 --> 00:11:16.975
He's like,
"Oh, you want something quiet

00:11:16.975 --> 00:11:19.511
and not too flashy.

00:11:19.511 --> 00:11:21.213
Get a Lexus."

00:11:21.213 --> 00:11:23.382
-"Get black."
-"Black."

00:11:23.382 --> 00:11:24.883
Yeah. But then --

00:11:24.883 --> 00:11:27.319
And then he gets it --
he's getting a new car,

00:11:27.319 --> 00:11:29.254
and he goes, "Which one
do you think I should get?

00:11:29.254 --> 00:11:31.823
The silver? Or the bl--"

00:11:31.823 --> 00:11:33.325
I go, "Really, James Bond?

00:11:33.325 --> 00:11:34.893
You want to drive around
in a silver car

00:11:34.893 --> 00:11:36.328
so everyone knows
where you are?"

00:11:36.328 --> 00:11:38.664
-He is kind of James Bond-y.
-He's like, "I know. I know."

00:11:38.664 --> 00:11:40.131
It's his own advice.
-Yeah.

00:11:40.131 --> 00:11:41.867
-But there's been
so many people.

00:11:41.867 --> 00:11:44.804
-Yeah.
-He's -- Lorne's been great.

00:11:44.804 --> 00:11:46.672
My grandparents
were really great to me.

00:11:46.672 --> 00:11:48.807
My grandma and my grandpa.
That was like --

00:11:48.807 --> 00:11:51.110
I used to go over there
after school every single day,

00:11:51.110 --> 00:11:53.178
and I just really hung out
with them.

00:11:53.178 --> 00:11:55.581
They really helped raise me.
-And by "over there," you mean?

00:11:55.581 --> 00:11:58.250
-They lived in my backyard.

00:11:58.250 --> 00:12:00.886
-Mm-hmm. [ Laughs ]
A cute little house

00:12:00.886 --> 00:12:02.988
behind your cute little house
up in Saugerties.

00:12:02.988 --> 00:12:04.757
-In Saugerties, New York.
They lived in our --

00:12:04.757 --> 00:12:05.925
Yeah.
-Mm-hmm.

00:12:05.925 --> 00:12:07.893
-It wasn't even, like,
a different address.

00:12:07.893 --> 00:12:09.294
-No, no.

00:12:09.294 --> 00:12:12.097
-Was it weird?
-No, I love it.

00:12:12.097 --> 00:12:13.999
-My grandparents
lived in our backyard.

00:12:13.999 --> 00:12:15.835
In a house. A cottage.
-Yeah.

00:12:15.835 --> 00:12:17.369
-So they used to babysit me,

00:12:17.369 --> 00:12:20.138
and I used to hang out with them
after school and stuff.

00:12:20.138 --> 00:12:21.707
-Yep. You used to cook
with your grandma.

00:12:21.707 --> 00:12:23.375
-I used to cook with my grandma
all the time

00:12:23.375 --> 00:12:24.977
and hang out, read cookbooks.

00:12:24.977 --> 00:12:28.113
-Go driving with your grandpa.
-I would go driving with them.

00:12:28.113 --> 00:12:31.082
And they would -- you know,
they would drink and drive.

00:12:31.082 --> 00:12:32.951
-Oh, well, those were
probably the days.

00:12:32.951 --> 00:12:34.620
-Different times.
-Different times.

00:12:34.620 --> 00:12:36.789
-They never told me. But they
would have, like, a Thermos.

00:12:36.789 --> 00:12:38.958
And then I remember once --

00:12:38.958 --> 00:12:41.226
-You thought it was --
You tried to try it.

00:12:41.226 --> 00:12:43.461
-Yeah. I tried to try it.
I go...

00:12:43.461 --> 00:12:45.164
-It's a dead giveaway. Parents.

00:12:45.164 --> 00:12:46.866
-Yeah, I go,
"Let me have a sip of it."

00:12:46.866 --> 00:12:48.800
They go, "No, no, no.
It's just orange juice."

00:12:48.800 --> 00:12:50.536
-"You won't like it."
-"Why are you guys

00:12:50.536 --> 00:12:52.538
drinking orange juice?
It's the afternoon.

00:12:52.538 --> 00:12:53.972
It's like 4:00
in the afternoon."

00:12:53.972 --> 00:12:55.707
They're like, "Ah, we're just
going for a drive, right?"

00:12:55.707 --> 00:12:59.311
Just -- [ Laughs ]
They wouldn't give me a sip.

00:12:59.311 --> 00:13:02.548
I was like, ah. You guys take
a little nip of a screwdriver...

00:13:02.548 --> 00:13:03.982
-Yep.

00:13:03.982 --> 00:13:05.484
-...with your grandkid
in the car.

00:13:05.484 --> 00:13:07.986
Probably no seatbelts.
-Teaching you how to drive.

00:13:07.986 --> 00:13:09.354
-[ Laughs ] All right.

00:13:09.354 --> 00:13:11.323
That's enough for
asking me questions.

00:13:11.323 --> 00:13:14.126
Now back to the show
that everyone wants to watch.

00:13:14.126 --> 00:13:15.895
[ Laughter ]

